# jobs — real-world examples

## List jobs in the current shell

```sh
jobs
jobs -l     # include PIDs
```

## Send a job to the background / bring to foreground

```sh
bg %1       # resume job 1 in the background
fg %1       # bring job 1 back to the foreground
```

## Suspend, background, and keep a job alive after exit

```sh
# Ctrl+Z suspends the foreground job
disown -h %1   # job won't get SIGHUP when the shell exits
bg %1          # resume it in the background
exit           # leave the shell without killing the job
# Ctrl+C kills the foreground job
# Closing the terminal window kills non-disowned background jobs
```

## Homelab: kick off a long rsync to mediaserver, then background it

```sh
rsync -avP ~/Videos/ david@mediaserver:/srv/plex/movies/
# Ctrl+Z, then:
bg %1
disown -h %1
```

## Homelab: start a command in the background from the start

```sh
nohup zypper dup --download-only > /tmp/dup.log 2>&1 &
jobs -l
```

## Homelab: better tool for long-running remote work — tmux

```sh
tmux new -s longjob    # detach: Ctrl+b d, reattach: tmux attach -t longjob
```

| Command / key | What it does                                |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| jobs -l       | list jobs with PIDs                         |
| Ctrl+Z        | suspend foreground job                      |
| bg %n         | resume job n in background                  |
| fg %n         | bring job n to foreground                   |
| disown -h %n  | protect job n from SIGHUP on shell exit     |
| nohup cmd &   | start immune to hangups, output to nohup.out|
